supersonic — source package layout
==================================

This source package uses format 3.0 (quilt) with three upstream tarballs:

  supersonic_<v>.orig.tar.xz
      The upstream repository (git archive of HEAD, which for a release is
      the commit tagged v<v>) with the clockwork git submodule archived into
      clockwork/ at the commit checked out there (the pinned one, in a clean
      checkout), minus the Files-Excluded entries listed in debian/copyright.
      Uncommitted changes are not included.

  supersonic_<v>.orig-link.tar.xz
      Pristine Ableton Link 4.1.1 including its asio-standalone submodule
      (the GitHub tag tarball does NOT contain the submodule, so this is
      assembled from a recursive clone). Unpacks to link/. clockwork's five
      Link patches (kept in clockwork/external/*.patch as the single source
      of truth) are applied to it via debian/patches/, path-shifted under
      link/.

  supersonic_<v>.orig-rust-vendor.tar.xz
      `cargo vendor` output for the committed clockwork/rust/Cargo.lock
      (clockwork's workspace; the scsynth guest is C++ and has no crates of
      its own). Unpacks to rust-vendor/; debian/rules points cargo at it and
      builds offline.

All three tarballs, the path-shifted patches, and a snapshot changelog entry
are generated by upstream's scripts/make-debian-source.sh (run by upstream CI
in .github/workflows/debian.yml, which then builds the package in a
network-disconnected container, runs lintian and autopkgtest). A maintainer
can reuse that script or assemble the components by hand as described above.

Upstream's script also assembles this package to build against the archive's
own crate packages instead of the rust-vendor component
(SUPERSONIC_DEB_CRATES=system scripts/make-debian-source.sh): no vendor
tarball, a generated patch reducing clockwork's Cargo manifests to Linux, and
a librust-<crate>-dev build dependency for each crate clockwork names.
debian/build-mode.mk, written by the script, records which form a source
package is. See "Building against the archive's own crates" in
docs/DEBIAN-PACKAGING.md.

Build dependencies come from the Debian archive: Catch2 is the system
package. The audio file decoders are Rust crates in the rust-vendor
component; the audio-device layer is clockwork's in-tree smoothie (an ISC
fork of four JUCE 7 modules); there is no JUCE build-dependency.
libsamplerate is the archive's (libsamplerate0-dev,
-DCLOCKWORK_SYSTEM_SAMPLERATE=ON); clockwork's own copy is left out of the
orig tarball. Plugin hosting (CLAP/VST3) is compiled
out because the SDKs are fetched at configure time and Debian ships neither.
See docs/DEBIAN-PACKAGING.md in the upstream tree for the full rationale and
the mapping table.
